    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1a7131f9-4aef-5e34-ae27-899d626b481b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 2 x^{2} + 2 y?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/workspace/evidence/cold-assets/17592186172040/plots/3c75d546e4b0a2ac278aeb10ee0fab62a727786c5bd2a49bc108ff6d334fe0c6.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/workspace/evidence/cold-assets/17592186172040/plots/4f825707713e7274a2379f28615cabb8b1eabdad24acbd8a3608bd3a3d821b25.png)",
